    "done_when": "resolve_launch_executable in reckon/crew/dispatch.py resolves the backend's own harness binary, the command after the fence's \"--\" separator in a fenced plan, and rewrites that element to its absolute path. The fence binary at argv[0] is never taken as the backend. A fenced plan whose backend binary is absent from the launch PATH is refused, naming the backend binary, before anything is written. An unfenced plan resolves exactly as it did before. The four tests reckon-bc measured failing at integrated head 4dcfb73a pass unchanged: tests/test_crew_launch_resolution.py::test_a_dispatch_plan_carries_an_absolute_executable, ::test_a_launcher_reached_through_a_symlink_keeps_its_own_name, ::test_a_dispatch_refuses_an_unresolvable_backend_before_writing_anything and ::test_a_resume_refuses_an_unresolvable_backend_and_leaves_the_pointer_alone. So do the other two in that file, and tests/test_crew_launch_resolution.py is not edited. A new test, tests/test_fenced_launch_resolves_the_backend.py, composes a fenced plan through the production launch path with a backend command absent from PATH. It asserts the refusal names that command and not the fence binary, and that a fenced plan with a present backend carries the backend's absolute path after the separator. The negative control reverts to reading argv[0], and the absent-backend case must fail by accepting the plan. tests/test_fence_is_on_again.py, tests/test_each_run_owns_its_harness_home.py and tests/test_fenced_resume_reaches_its_harness.py add no failures against the base, with pytest basetemp outside any git repository. Gate and control logs are written under the run directory. The change is committed in this worktree.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Make launch resolution resolve the backend harness binary inside a fenced plan rather than the fence binary at argv[0], so a missing backend is refused again.",
